values.value:
== LIGHTNING DATA SERVICE (RECORD APIs / CRUD) ==
Use when: the ticket needs to read, create, update, or delete a Salesforce
record directly from a component's own data — no custom Apex controller
required for plain CRUD against fields the running user already has access
to. Prefer LDS over a custom Apex method whenever the operation is a plain
field read/write — LDS gets you sharing/FLS enforcement, the shared record
cache, and offline draft support for free; a custom Apex method for the
same job is extra surface with none of that benefit.

-- READING A RECORD: @wire(getRecord) + getFieldValue --
Always import field references from '@salesforce/schema/Object.Field' —
never hardcode a field's API name as a bare string:

  import { LightningElement, api, wire } from 'lwc';
  import { getRecord, getFieldValue } from 'lightning/uiRecordApi';
  import NAME_FIELD from '@salesforce/schema/Contact.Name';
  import EMAIL_FIELD from '@salesforce/schema/Contact.Email';

  export default class ContactCard extends LightningElement {
      @api recordId;

      @wire(getRecord, { recordId: '$recordId', fields: [NAME_FIELD, EMAIL_FIELD] })
      contact;

      get name() {
          return getFieldValue(this.contact.data, NAME_FIELD);
      }
      get email() {
          return getFieldValue(this.contact.data, EMAIL_FIELD);
      }
  }

CRITICAL RULE: @wire(getRecord, {...}) MUST supply at least one of fields,
optionalFields, or layoutTypes — omitting all three throws at runtime
("either fields or layout type must be specified"), not at deploy time.

CRITICAL RULE: getRecord is a WIRE ADAPTER ONLY — it can never be called
as a plain imperative function (getRecord({...}) with no @wire), even
inside a .then()/async block, e.g. after a save completes. Doing so is a
compile-time error: LWC1503 "getRecord is a wire adapter and can only be
used via the @wire decorator." This is DIFFERENT from
createRecord/updateRecord/deleteRecord/getFieldValue, which genuinely ARE
plain functions callable imperatively — getRecord is the one exception in
this module. If a handler needs to re-fetch/refresh a record's data after
a write (e.g. to show updated values after Save), do NOT call
getRecord(...) directly — instead call refreshApex(wiredResult) on the
component's existing @wire(getRecord) result property, or simply rely on
the wire's own reactivity (it re-runs automatically whenever its reactive
'$recordId' parameter changes). refreshApex is imported from
'@salesforce/apex', not 'lightning/uiRecordApi'.

RULES:
- '$recordId' (with the leading $) reactively re-runs the wire whenever
  the recordId property changes — a plain recordId: this.recordId does
  NOT do this and only fires once.
- Always guard getFieldValue/rendering on this.contact.data being present
  before the wire resolves (e.g. <template lwc:if={contact.data}>) —
  contact.data is undefined on the very first render.
- Never mutate this.contact.data directly to reflect an edit — it is
  owned by the LDS wire service. Call updateRecord(...) (see below) and
  let the wire naturally refetch, or call refreshApex(this.contact).
- fields/optionalFields entries are imported schema references (NAME_FIELD),
  never raw strings like 'Contact.Name' — EXCEPT the genuinely dynamic,
  runtime-configurable case below, where a schema import is impossible.

-- DYNAMICALLY-CONFIGURABLE FIELDS (fields list driven by @api input) --
Use when: the ticket wants the set of fields to be configuration — passed
in through an @api property, built from user selection, or otherwise not
knowable until runtime — rather than a fixed list the component always
requests.

A `@salesforce/schema/Object.Field` import is resolved at BUILD time, so it
CANNOT represent a field decided at RUN time. For this case only, fall back
to plain strings — but every string MUST be the FULLY OBJECT-QUALIFIED API
name, `"ObjectApiName.FieldApiName"` (e.g. `'Contact.Email'`,
`'Account.Industry'`), never a bare field name (`'Email'`). getRecord's
fields array requires the qualified form unconditionally — a bare name
throws at runtime ("field API name ... is not valid") with no deploy-time
or static-validation signal, since a dynamically-built array is exactly
the shape the literal-only validators (updateRecord Id, createRecord
apiName, getRecord fields/layoutTypes) intentionally do not inspect.

  import { LightningElement, api, wire, track } from 'lwc';
  import { getRecord, getFieldValue } from 'lightning/uiRecordApi';

  export default class ConfigurableFieldDisplay extends LightningElement {
      @api recordId;
      @api fieldApiNames = []; // e.g. ['Contact.Email', 'Contact.Phone'] — caller-supplied, object-qualified

      @wire(getRecord, { recordId: '$recordId', fields: '$fieldApiNames' })
      record;

      get displayFields() {
          if (!this.record.data || !this.fieldApiNames) {
              return [];
          }
          return this.fieldApiNames.map((apiName) => ({
              apiName,
              value: getFieldValue(this.record.data, apiName)
          }));
      }
  }

RULES:
- The `fields`/`optionalFields` wire parameter itself may also be reactive
  ('$fieldApiNames') so the wire re-runs if the caller changes the field
  list after initial render — not required, but never wrong.
- getFieldValue accepts a plain qualified string directly (no imported
  token needed) — `getFieldValue(this.record.data, 'Contact.Email')` is
  correct and is the intended dynamic-case usage.
- If the ticket implies a caller might pass unqualified names, still emit
  the component expecting qualified input and document it in a comment —
  never silently prepend a guessed object name; the component cannot know
  which object a bare field name belongs to.
- Guard on `this.record.data` (or `this.record.error`) exactly as the
  static case does — the dynamic field list changes what's fetched, not
  the loading/error-guard shape.

-- CRITICAL: getRecord's layoutTypes DOES NOT RETURN LAYOUT METADATA --
layoutTypes on getRecord ONLY changes which fields are populated inside
record.fields (Compact = the compact layout's fields, Full = the full
layout's fields) — the RESPONSE SHAPE IS STILL THE PLAIN RecordRepresentation
({ apiName, fields, id, ... }). getRecord's response NEVER has a `layouts`
property, no matter what layoutTypes says. Confirmed as a real incident
2026-08-25 (caseFullLayoutPanel): code read `record.layouts.Full.sections`
off a getRecord result — record.layouts was always undefined, so the
component silently rendered an empty card on every load, with no error
and no deploy-time signal anything was wrong.

If the ticket needs the actual rendered layout — sections, rows, per-field
labels grouped the way Setup's page layout defines them — that data comes
from a COMPLETELY DIFFERENT wire adapter, getRecordUi (also from
'lightning/uiRecordApi'), whose response genuinely is
{ records, layouts, objectInfos }:

  import { getRecordUi } from 'lightning/uiRecordApi';

  @wire(getRecordUi, { recordIds: ['$recordId'], layoutTypes: ['Full'], modes: ['View'] })
  wiredRecordUi({ data, error }) {
      if (data) {
          const record = data.records[this.recordId];
          const sections = data.layouts[record.apiName].View.Full.sections;
          // sections[].layoutRows[].layoutItems[] — this is the real shape
          // that carries section headings/rows/fields, and it ONLY exists
          // on a getRecordUi response.
      }
  }

STRONGLY PREFERRED for "show a record's full/compact layout" tickets: skip
hand-rolled getRecordUi extraction entirely and use the built-in
<lightning-record-form> component instead — it renders the requested
layout (including every section and field) with zero custom wire/JS code:

  <lightning-record-form
      record-id={recordId}
      object-api-name="Case"
      layout-type="Full"
      columns="2">
  </lightning-record-form>

Reach for getRecordUi directly only when the ticket needs to read layout
metadata programmatically (e.g. to drive custom conditional logic per
section) — not merely to display it, which lightning-record-form already
does correctly out of the box.

-- WRITING A RECORD: updateRecord / createRecord / deleteRecord --
All three come from 'lightning/uiRecordApi' and return a Promise —
combine with the isLoading spinner and ShowToastEvent patterns from
LWC_NAVIGATION (mandatory feedback on every write, spinner always reset
in .finally()):

  import { updateRecord, createRecord, deleteRecord } from 'lightning/uiRecordApi';

  // UPDATE — fields MUST include Id, or LDS throws at runtime:
  updateRecord({ fields: { Id: this.recordId, Name: this.nameValue } })
      .then(() => { /* toast success */ })
      .catch((error) => { /* toast error */ })
      .finally(() => { this.isLoading = false; });

  // CREATE — the record-input object MUST include apiName, or LDS throws
  // at runtime ("Cannot destructure property 'apiName'"):
  createRecord({ apiName: 'Contact', fields: { LastName: this.lastName } })
      .then((record) => { this.newRecordId = record.id; /* toast success */ })
      .catch((error) => { /* toast error */ })
      .finally(() => { this.isLoading = false; });

  // DELETE — takes the record Id directly, not a fields object:
  deleteRecord(this.recordId)
      .then(() => { /* toast success */ })
      .catch((error) => { /* toast error */ })
      .finally(() => { this.isLoading = false; });

RULES:
- Every one of createRecord/updateRecord/deleteRecord/getFieldValue used
  in a file MUST be imported by name from 'lightning/uiRecordApi' — none
  are globals; an unimported call fails at RUNTIME ("X is not defined"),
  not at deploy time.
- updateRecord's fields object ALWAYS includes Id — it identifies which
  record to write to.
- createRecord's top-level object ALWAYS includes apiName — it identifies
  which object to insert into.
- Do NOT write a custom Apex method that performs its own DML
  (insert/update/upsert/delete) for the SAME write a component already
  performs via LDS — pick ONE path. Reach for a custom Apex method only
  when the write needs logic LDS cannot express (cross-object DML in one
  transaction, a validation/business rule not enforceable via a single
  record write, a bulk operation).
  EXCEPTION — this does NOT forbid a Save handler that calls both an LDS
  write AND an imperative Apex method, when the Apex method's DML target
  is genuinely different from what LDS already wrote (e.g. LDS updates
  Rating; a separate Apex method writes an audit/log record and a
  Last_Modified/timestamp field as a side effect of that change). That is
  two distinct writes for two distinct purposes, not the same write done
  twice — see "COMBINING AN LDS WRITE WITH AN IMPERATIVE APEX CALL" below
  for the required control-flow shape.

-- COMBINING AN LDS WRITE WITH AN IMPERATIVE APEX CALL IN ONE HANDLER --
Use when: the ticket needs a Save handler to perform an LDS write (e.g.
updateRecord) AND separately invoke an imperative Apex method (e.g. for
audit logging, a side-effect timestamp field, or any action LDS cannot
itself express) as part of the same user action.

Chaining two async write calls with nested .then()/.catch() is exactly the
shape that has repeatedly produced a spinner-never-resets bug in practice
— it is easy to place .finally() on only one of the two calls, or to
return early from one .catch() in a way that skips the other's cleanup.
For ANY handler with two or more sequential async write operations, use
async/await with ONE try/catch/finally wrapping the entire sequence —
this guarantees isLoading resets exactly once, regardless of which step
throws:

  import { LightningElement, api, track, wire } from 'lwc';
  import { getRecord, getFieldValue, updateRecord } from 'lightning/uiRecordApi';
  import { refreshApex } from '@salesforce/apex';
  import { ShowToastEvent } from 'lightning/platformShowToastEvent';
  import RATING_FIELD from '@salesforce/schema/Account.Rating';
  import logRatingChange from '@salesforce/apex/AccountAuditLogger.logRatingChange';

  export default class AccountRatingUpdater extends LightningElement {
      @api recordId;
      @track isLoading = false;
      @track ratingValue;

      // Reading the CURRENT value (to seed the input) is a @wire, never an
      // imperative getRecord(...) call — see the CRITICAL RULE above.
      @wire(getRecord, { recordId: '$recordId', fields: [RATING_FIELD] })
      account;

      get currentRating() {
          return getFieldValue(this.account.data, RATING_FIELD);
      }

      async handleSave() {
          if (this.isLoading) {
              return;
          }
          this.isLoading = true;
          try {
              await updateRecord({ fields: { Id: this.recordId, Rating: this.ratingValue } });
              await logRatingChange({ acc: { Id: this.recordId, Rating: this.ratingValue } });
              // Re-fetch to reflect the change — refreshApex on the EXISTING
              // wired result, never a fresh imperative getRecord(...) call.
              await refreshApex(this.account);
              this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({
                  title: 'Success',
                  message: 'Rating updated.',
                  variant: 'success'
              }));
          } catch (error) {
              this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({
                  title: 'Error updating rating',
                  message: getErrorMessage(error),
                  variant: 'error'
              }));
          } finally {
              this.isLoading = false;
          }
      }
  }

RULES:
- One try/catch/finally around the WHOLE sequence of awaited calls — never
  a separate .then/.catch/.finally per call when there is more than one
  write in the handler.
- Need the record's current value, before or after the write? Use
  @wire(getRecord) + refreshApex(wiredResult) — never a bare getRecord(...)
  call anywhere in the file, imperative or otherwise.
- isLoading = false lives ONLY in the single finally block — never
  duplicated inside then/catch as well, which invites drift if one copy
  is edited later and the other is not.
- Route the caught error through getErrorMessage(error) (see
  LWC_NAVIGATION's error-extraction rule) regardless of which of the two
  calls actually threw — the helper handles both an LDS error shape and
  an AuraHandledException shape the same way.
- The imperative Apex method itself stays plain @AuraEnabled (performs
  DML) — never cacheable=true — per the standard Tier 2B DML rules.

== NEVER ==
- Never call @wire(getRecord, {...}) without fields, optionalFields, or
  layoutTypes — it throws at runtime with nothing rendered.
- Never read a `.layouts` property off a getRecord wire result (or off
  getFieldValue's record argument) — getRecord's response never has one,
  even when layoutTypes is set. That shape only exists on getRecordUi's
  response. For displaying a full/compact layout, prefer
  <lightning-record-form layout-type="Full"|"Compact"> over hand-rolled
  extraction; use getRecordUi directly only when the layout metadata must
  drive custom logic.
- Never call updateRecord with a fields object that omits Id.
- Never call createRecord with a record-input object that omits apiName.
- Never call createRecord/updateRecord/deleteRecord/getFieldValue without
  importing it from 'lightning/uiRecordApi'.
- Never hardcode a field's API name as a bare string where an imported
  '@salesforce/schema/Object.Field' reference belongs.
- Never build a custom Apex DML method AND call an LDS write function for
  the same record write in the same component — pick one path. (This does
  NOT forbid an LDS write plus a genuinely separate imperative Apex call —
  e.g. an audit log / side-effect timestamp field — see "COMBINING AN LDS
  WRITE WITH AN IMPERATIVE APEX CALL IN ONE HANDLER" above.)
- Never chain two or more sequential async write calls (LDS + Apex, or
  Apex + Apex) with separate .then()/.catch()/.finally() blocks each —
  use one async/await try/catch/finally around the whole sequence so
  isLoading resets exactly once no matter which call fails.
